<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Additional Backgrounds</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the advancement of deep learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2025d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2025e</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025e</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025f</a>; <a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">JiangYong Yu &amp; Yuan., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2024d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Niu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kudrat et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2024d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conde et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Di et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ignatov et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., </a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., </a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yakovenko et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qu et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lyu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen &amp; Greer, 2025</a>)</cite> and generative models, an increasing number of studies have begun to focus on the issue of concept erasure in generative models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Additional Experimental Details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation metrics details:</span> For CLIP similarity, we used the ViT-L/14 model to compute image-text cosine similarity, scaled by 100. The original SD1.5 had an average CLIP score of 31.5 on MS-COCO validation prompts; after concept erasure, we consider a score above 30 to indicate minimal drop in alignment. Harmonic mean <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> was computed as described with <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=1-\text{Acc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext>Acc</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=1-\text{Acc}</annotation></semantics></math> (normalized to [0,1]) and <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> composed from FID and CLIP. Specifically, we defined <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=\frac{1}{2}((\frac{\text{CLIP sim}}{\text{CLIP}_{\text{orig}}})+(\frac{\max(\text{FID}_{\text{orig}}-(\text{FID}-\text{FID}_{\text{orig}}),0)}{\text{FID}_{\text{orig}}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mtext>CLIP sim</mtext><msub><mtext>CLIP</mtext><mtext>orig</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>FID</mtext><mtext>orig</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>FID</mtext><mo>−</mo><msub><mtext>FID</mtext><mtext>orig</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mtext>FID</mtext><mtext>orig</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=\frac{1}{2}((\frac{\text{CLIP sim}}{\text{CLIP}_{\text{orig}}})+(\frac{\max(\text{FID}_{\text{orig}}-(\text{FID}-\text{FID}_{\text{orig}}),0)}{\text{FID}_{\text{orig}}}))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FID}_{\text{orig}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>FID</mtext><mtext>orig</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FID}_{\text{orig}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CLIP}_{\text{orig}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>CLIP</mtext><mtext>orig</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CLIP}_{\text{orig}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the original model’s scores (so we reward methods that keep FID low and CLIP high relative to orig). This is one way; results were qualitatively similar with other formulations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-concept results:</span> We erased all 10 CIFAR classes simultaneously with FADE by using a 10-way classifier <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> (one output per class vs no class). FADE achieved an average concept accuracy of 1.1% per class and an overall <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=82.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>82.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=82.3</annotation></semantics></math> (versus MACE’s reported  75). The slight residual is due to class confusion (e.g., sometimes after erasure ”cat” prompt yields a dog, so classifier might say cat=present when it sees an animal shape; a limitation of using automated classifier for eval). Visual check showed indeed direct appearance of the specified class was gone. For NSFW, we erased 10 terms at once; here FADE and MACE both got basically 0% unsafe content, but FADE had better image quality (FID 14 vs 16).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Runtime:</span> FADE training takes about 2 hours on a single A100 GPU for a single concept on SD1.5 (with <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1000</annotation></semantics></math> steps adversarial training). This is comparable to ESD fine-tuning time and a bit less than ANT . UCE was fastest (minutes) as it is closed-form. There’s room to optimize FADE’s training, possibly by using smaller <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> or gradient accumulation. Deploying FADE in multi-concept setting could be parallelized since the adversary can output multiple heads.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
